From d0b3661a78aeb70c2905bd99537cc55c727bc00f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leonardo Tada Antunes <leonardotada@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 18:26:01 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/20] Mostly-concurrent mark: the program runs while the
 collector marks (opt-in)

SYMGC_CONCURRENT_MARK=1, Linux 6.7 or later. A GC thread runs the whole cycle;
the thread that triggered it goes on. Only two short pauses stop the program:

- start pause: prepare the mark bits, empty the bins, start an epoch, and list
  the roots, stacks and thread local storage as work (the ranges are read, and
  traced, after the world restarts);
- remark pause: scan the roots, stacks and thread locals again, and the pages
  the program wrote since the last round.

The kernel tells us which pages were written (userfaultfd WP_ASYNC and
PAGEMAP_SCAN, d.gc.dirty), so no compiler support is needed: any D, C or
kernel write is seen. This is Boehm, Demers and Shenker's mostly-concurrent
collector. A pointer stored into an object the marker already scanned dirties
its page; the stacks and roots are scanned last, with the world stopped.

Other rules it needs: extents and blocks born since the mark started are live
and skipped by the mark (tagged with a 32 bit epoch, not the 8 bit cycle: a
stale tag would make the mark skip a live object); large extents are not
freed or resized in place while the mark runs, and slabs are owned by the
sweep; allocating threads wait when the heap passes the hard limit during the
mark; and if the kernel stops tracking, the remark marks everything again with
the world stopped (a new epoch, so the born extents are ordinary ones).

mark_race.d: a graph whose pointers are moved through locals while collections
run. 0 damaged nodes in 1.1M moves; with the dirty rescan disabled
(SYMGC_DIRTY_SKIP=1, planted bug) 3202; with tracking forced to fail
(SYMGC_DIRTY_FAIL=1) the fallback keeps it at 0.

frames, worst frame: 512 MiB 10.7 -> 1.8 ms, 2 GiB 31.0 -> 3.5 ms.
